1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 30: cables and VNA measurements

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:18,522
YA-NEC course, lesson 30: cables and measurements. Calculators, Tools, RG cables: losses from our cable chart.

3
00:00:18,572 --> 00:00:32,343
Example: 20 m of RG-59 at 144.3 MHz into a 50 ohm load. 13.5 dB per 100 m: 2.7 dB loss, 52 % reaches the load.

4
00:00:32,393 --> 00:00:42,913
The 75 ohm cable on a 50 ohm load transforms the impedance: 66.6 + j12.8 ohm at the transmitter, SWR 1.24.

5
00:00:42,963 --> 00:00:50,046
On the right, the impedance change with a length of cable: a 100 ohm antenna to bring to 50.

6
00:00:50,096 --> 00:01:05,161
It needs a Z0 of 70.7 ohm: a quarter wave of RG-59, 34.3 cm with the 0.66 velocity factor, gives 56.4 ohm. SWR 1.13 instead of 2.

7
00:01:05,211 --> 00:01:13,694
Below, the other possible transformers, best first: one click picks one, and the line can go into the open model.

8
00:01:13,744 --> 00:01:44,017
Now measurements. From the Examples the 144 MHz dipole; Patterns, VNA measurements.

9
00:01:44,067 --> 00:02:07,738
Open measurement reads a Touchstone .s1p file or the NanoVNA-Saver CSV. Here a sample file made for this lesson, not a real measurement.

10
00:02:07,788 --> 00:02:15,271
In red the measured SWR, in blue the NEC calculation; on the right both curves on the Smith chart.

11
00:02:15,321 --> 00:02:27,114
The comparison: calculated SWR minimum at 141.66 MHz, measured at 143.2. The measurement is 1.09 % higher.

12
00:02:27,164 --> 00:02:36,914
The proposal: lengthen the driven element by 5.5 mm per side, or turn on the boom correction. The program changes nothing by itself.

13
00:02:36,964 --> 00:02:44,648
On the left you remove the cable between VNA and antenna from the measurement: cable type and length.

14
00:02:44,698 --> 00:02:56,171
Sources: W4ZT and YB0EMJ for the cables, the Touchstone format for VNA files. Next lesson: more calculators. 73 de IC8TEM
